---

<DocsLayout
  title="Canvas - Arris Docs"
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Docs", href: "/" }, { label: "Canvas" }]}
  heading="Canvas"
  lede="An infinite board that holds an analysis as connected objects: SQL queries that run, charts bound to their results, and the notes that frame them. Build each object by hand, or ask a coding agent to assemble it."
  tocItems={[
    { label: "Why use it", href: "#why" },
    { label: "Open a canvas", href: "#open" },
    { label: "Connections", href: "#connections" },
    { label: "Build with a coding agent", href: "#coding-agents" },
    { label: "Build it by hand", href: "#manual" },
    { label: "Object kinds", href: "#objects" },
    { label: "The properties pane", href: "#properties" },
    { label: "Working on the board", href: "#board" },
    { label: "Saving", href: "#saving" },
  ]}
  currentPath="/canvas"
  prev={{ label: "Trino", href: "/supported-data-sources/trino", section: "Supported data sources" }}
  next={{ label: "Editor", href: "/querying/editor", section: "Querying" }}
>
  <h2 id="why">Why use it</h2>

  <p>
    A query tab answers one question and scrolls away. A canvas keeps the whole analysis in view:
    the SQL that pulls the data, the chart that makes it readable, and the notes that explain it,
    arranged side by side on an infinite board you pan and zoom. Reach for a canvas to:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Go from a question to a chart fast.</strong> Ask Claude Code or Codex for "monthly sales by category" and it writes the query, adds the chart, and both run on the board.</li>
    <li><strong>Assemble a dashboard-style view.</strong> Several queries, charts, and tables laid out together and refreshed on demand.</li>
    <li><strong>Explore step by step.</strong> Chain one query off another's results, branch into variants, and annotate as you go.</li>
    <li><strong>Work across databases.</strong> Give each query cell its own connection and combine their results with <code>FROM &lt;cell title&gt;</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="open">Open a canvas</h2>

  <p>There are two ways to start a board:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Click <strong>New Canvas</strong>, the sparkles button
      <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="inline-block align-text-bottom text-accent-2"><path d="M9.937 15.5A2 2 0 0 0 8.5 14.063l-6.135-1.582a.5.5 0 0 1 0-.962L8.5 9.936A2 2 0 0 0 9.937 8.5l1.582-6.135a.5.5 0 0 1 .963 0L14.063 8.5A2 2 0 0 0 15.5 9.937l6.135 1.581a.5.5 0 0 1 0 .964L15.5 14.063a2 2 0 0 0-1.437 1.437l-1.582 6.135a.5.5 0 0 1-.963 0z" /><path d="M20 3v4" /><path d="M22 5h-4" /><path d="M4 17v2" /><path d="M5 18H3" /></svg>,
      in the editor tab bar.
    </li>
    <li>Right-click an empty area of the <strong>Canvas</strong> section in the left sidebar and choose <strong>New Canvas</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A canvas opens as an ordinary editor tab (not a separate window), auto-titled <code>Canvas 1</code>,
    <code>Canvas 2</code>, and so on, with the board filling the tab. Rename a board by double-clicking its row in the sidebar
    Canvas list, or right-click the row and choose <strong>Rename</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="connections">Connections</h2>

  <p>
    Every query cell stores its own connection, which you change in the properties pane, so one
    board can mix databases. A new query cell uses the connection you last picked on the board, else
    the newest query cell's, else the connection the editor had selected when the board opened.
  </p>

  <h2 id="coding-agents">Build with a coding agent</h2>

  <p>
    With a <a href="/ai/coding-agents">coding agent</a> (Claude Code or Codex), ask for a dashboard or for a result on a board,
    for example <em>"put monthly sales by category on a canvas as a line chart"</em>. The agent runs the
    query, then adds it to a new or existing board below its lowest object, with a chart bound to it
    when you asked for one. The open Arris tab switches to the board, which runs its read queries and
    draws the chart; a write waits for you to press Run.
  </p>

  <p>
    Follow-ups change the board in place: the agent can redraw a chart with another type or axes, remove
    an object and its arrows, or delete the whole canvas when you ask. Under the hood these are the
    <code>arris canvas</code> commands, listed in the <a href="/ai/coding-agents#cli">CLI reference</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="manual">Build it by hand</h2>

  <p>
    Every object can be added directly. The toolbar at the bottom of the board
    has a button for each kind; selecting one places a new object in the center of your current view, so
    it stays in sight however far you have panned.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="/screenshots/canvas-toolbar.png"
    alt="The canvas toolbar: a pointer tool with a caret, then query cell, chart, table, sticky note, text, and shape buttons (the shape button has a caret), and a run-all button at the far right."
    caption="The board toolbar. The pointer and shape tools have a caret that opens a menu of options above the bar."
  />

  <DocTable>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Tool</th>
        <th>What it does</th>
        <th>Shortcut</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Pointer</td>
        <td>The caret opens two modes: <strong>Move</strong> (drag and select objects, the default) and the <strong>Hand</strong> tool (drag anywhere to pan the board without moving objects).</td>
        <td><kbd>V</kbd> / <kbd>H</kbd></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Query cell</td>
        <td>Adds a SQL cell bound to the board's primary connection.</td>
        <td><kbd>/</kbd></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Chart</td>
        <td>Adds a chart. Pick its source query in the properties pane.</td>
        <td>None</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Table</td>
        <td>Adds a preview table. Pick its source query in the properties pane.</td>
        <td>None</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Sticky note</td>
        <td>Adds a coloured annotation note.</td>
        <td>None</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Text</td>
        <td>Adds a free text block for headings and commentary.</td>
        <td>None</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Shape</td>
        <td>The caret chooses <strong>Rectangle</strong>, <strong>Ellipse</strong>, or <strong>Line</strong>.</td>
        <td><kbd>R</kbd> / <kbd>O</kbd> / <kbd>L</kbd></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Run all</td>
        <td>Runs every query on the board, resolving each query's upstream cells first.</td>
        <td>None</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </DocTable>

  <p>
    The pointer and shape tools remember your last choice: clicking the main button re-applies it and
    opens the menu, so picking a different option is one more click. The single-key shortcuts fire only
    while the board is focused, never while you are typing in a query cell or a text block, and every one
    is rebindable under Settings &rsaquo; Keymap.
  </p>

  <h2 id="objects">Object kinds</h2>

  <p>Every object can be positioned, sized, locked, and overlapped freely. What each one does:</p>

  <DocTable>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Object</th>
        <th>What it does</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Query cell</td>
        <td>
          A SQL statement in the same dialect-aware, schema-completing editor as the main SQL editor,
          with a <strong>Run</strong> button in its header and a one-line status below ("Running...",
          "<em>N</em> rows &middot; <em>N</em> columns", an error, or "Run the query to preview data").
          While a run is in flight the Run button becomes a <strong>Cancel</strong> button that stops
          the query.
          The cell shows no grid itself: on its first successful run a preview <strong>table</strong> is
          auto-placed beside it and joined with an arrow, unless a table or chart is already bound to it.
          Reference another cell by title with <code>FROM &lt;cell title&gt;</code> to build on its
          result, or wrap the title in backticks (<code>FROM `Query 1`</code>) to write it exactly as
          it appears on the board. A new cell is named <code>Query 1</code>, <code>Query 2</code> and
          so on, so it can be referenced right away; rename it in the properties pane.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Table</td>
        <td>Previews a query's rows in the same grid as the results viewer: sortable columns, in-view search, a JSON row-detail pane, and CSV/JSON download, with a refresh button that re-runs the source query. A footer shows the row and column counts, the page, and the last-refresh time. Shows up to 200 rows per page by default (configurable) and pages through the full result. While the source is still running it shows a spinner, not a partial page.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Chart</td>
        <td>Visualizes a query's result with the same chart editor as the results viewer (type, axes, series, aggregation, colours, legend, labels), refreshed whenever that query runs. A title bar shows the chart's name (defaulting to the source query's) with a refresh button that re-runs the source query, and any query or chart error surfaces in a status bar in red along the bottom. While the source is loading the chart icon spins.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Text</td>
        <td>A free text block for headings and commentary. Single-click selects and drags; double-click to edit. Font size, run styles (bold, italic, underline, strikethrough), alignment, and text and background colours are set in the properties pane.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Sticky note</td>
        <td>Free text on a coloured card pinned on top of the board. Double-click to edit; pick one of five tints (yellow, green, blue, pink, purple).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="font-medium text-fg">Shape</td>
        <td>A rectangle, ellipse, or line for grouping and annotation. Rectangles and ellipses hold an optional centered label (double-click to add it); a rectangle's corners round by dragging the radius handle on its top edge; a line can be solid, dashed, or dotted.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </DocTable>

  <h2 id="properties">The properties pane</h2>

  <p>
    Select a single object and a properties pane opens on the right. Every kind shows
    <strong>X</strong>, <strong>Y</strong>, <strong>W</strong>, and <strong>H</strong> inputs and a
    <strong>Locked</strong> toggle; editing them moves or resizes the object live. Below that, each kind
    has its own controls:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Query:</strong> title, connection, and a row <strong>Limit</strong> for the fetch (default 500), with a <strong>Select all rows</strong> toggle that fetches the full result instead. Changes apply on the next run. (The SQL is edited in the cell body.)</li>
    <li><strong>Table:</strong> source query and a preview-row cap (blank uses the default of 200).</li>
    <li><strong>Chart:</strong> source query and a row cap (blank uses the default of 1000), then the full chart editor: type, axes (X/Y number formatting to abbreviate large values, decimals, a prefix/suffix like <code>$</code> or <code>%</code>, axis width, horizontal padding so long labels are not clipped, tick density, and label angles), appearance, legend, and colours, driven from the bound query's last run.</li>
    <li><strong>Shape:</strong> fill colour (rectangles and ellipses), stroke colour and width, line style (solid, dashed, dotted), and corner radius (rectangles).</li>
    <li><strong>Text:</strong> font size, run styles (bold, italic, underline, strikethrough), alignment (left, center, right), and text and background colours.</li>
    <li><strong>Sticky note:</strong> the tint.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="board">Working on the board</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Select and move.</strong> Single-click an object to select it; drag to move it (in Move mode). Selection drives the resize handles and the properties pane and persists until you click an empty part of the board.</li>
    <li><strong>Resize.</strong> Drag any border or corner handle of a selected object. Handles only appear while it is selected.</li>
    <li><strong>Overlap and reorder.</strong> Objects may overlap. Right-click for <strong>Bring to front</strong> (<kbd>]</kbd>), <strong>Bring forward</strong>, <strong>Send backward</strong>, and <strong>Send to back</strong> (<kbd>[</kbd>).</li>
    <li><strong>Copy, paste, lock, delete.</strong> <kbd>&#8984;C</kbd> copies the selected object to an in-app clipboard and <kbd>&#8984;V</kbd> pastes a clone nearby; <strong>Lock</strong> freezes an object (still selectable, but not movable or resizable until unlocked); <kbd>Delete</kbd> or <kbd>Backspace</kbd> removes it. All are also in the right-click menu.</li>
    <li><strong>Pan and zoom.</strong> Pan with the Hand tool or by dragging an empty part of the board; zoom with the scroll wheel or the controls in the corner (0.2x to 2x). Scrolling inside a table, chart, or query cell scrolls that object, not the board.</li>
    <li><strong>Dependency arrows are automatic.</strong> Arrows are drawn when a chart or table binds to its source query, and when one query reads another with <code>FROM &lt;cell title&gt;</code> (backticks around the title work too). They anchor to both objects' borders and re-route as you move them. You cannot draw them by hand, but you can delete one from its right-click menu.</li>
    <li><strong>Run.</strong> Run a single query with its Run button (or the editor's run shortcut); while it runs the button becomes <strong>Cancel</strong> and stops the query. <strong>Run all</strong> on the toolbar runs every query, resolving upstream cells first.</li>
    <li><strong>Fit on open.</strong> Reopening a board frames its existing content, so you land on your work rather than empty space.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="saving">Saving</h2>

  <p>
    A board is saved with your workspace shortly after each change, and survives closing the tab or
    restarting the app. It stores the objects (their geometry, z-order, lock state, and per-kind
    settings), the dependency arrows, and the pan and zoom.
    <strong>Query results are not saved.</strong> After reopening, query cells show their "Run the
    query..." state, and tables and charts stay blank until you re-run their source query (or use
    <strong>Run all</strong>).
  </p>

  <p>
    Closed boards live in the <strong>Canvas</strong> section of the left sidebar, newest first. Click a
    row to reopen it, double-click to rename, or right-click to rename, move to a project, or delete (a
    delete can be restored).
  </p>

</DocsLayout>
